    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-04",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A maximum thermometer records the highest temperature reached since its last reset, and a minimum thermometer the lowest. A Six’s thermometer combines both in one U-shaped glass tube. Alcohol in the tube expands and contracts as the temperature changes and pushes a thread of mercury round the bend. The mercury pushes a small steel index along each arm, and each index stays at the furthest point it was pushed to. The maximum is read on the right-hand arm, whose scale reads upwards; the minimum on the left-hand arm, whose scale reads downwards. Read the end of each index nearest the mercury, with your eye level with it. The top of the mercury on either scale gives the current temperature. After recording, reset the thermometer by drawing each index back down onto the mercury with a magnet, or by pressing the release button on newer models. A missed reset changes the period that the next reading covers, so note it in the record.",
