    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-06",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A wet-and-dry-bulb hygrometer, or psychrometer, uses paired thermometers. The wet bulb has a covering supplied with water; evaporation removes heat and can cool it below the dry bulb. The difference depends on humidity and ventilation. In drier air, evaporation is generally greater under comparable conditions, producing a larger depression. If wet and dry readings coincide under the appropriate functioning conditions, the air is saturated and relative humidity is 100%. A dry wick or poor ventilation can undermine the inference, so equality of readings should not be interpreted without considering the instrument’s condition.",
      "claim_ids": [
