    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-08",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A barometer measures atmospheric pressure. It can use a liquid column or a calibrated sensor such as an aneroid mechanism. A barograph records pressure over time, historically with a pen and chart and in modern systems often digitally. A single barometer reading gives pressure at that observation; a recording trace shows changes during a period. Check whether values are station pressure or adjusted to a reference level when comparing places of different altitude. Otherwise a difference due to elevation can be mistaken for the same kind of weather-system contrast shown on a sea-level pressure chart.",
