    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-09",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Pressure is force per unit area, not a temperature scale. A barometer reading of 1,010 hPa does not mean that the air is 1,010 degrees or that a lower pressure centre must have lower air temperature than every surrounding place. A falling trace can be relevant to an approaching change in conditions, but it does not alone establish the amount or timing of rainfall. The wider system, moisture and air motion matter. A barograph resource should have legible pressure and time axes, a stated interval and a clear meaning for any reference adjustment.",
