    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-10",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "An anemometer measures wind speed. A common cup design rotates as air moves past it, and the rotation is converted through calibration to speed. Different designs use other sensing methods, so cups are a recognisable example rather than the definition. Appropriate exposure matters: a sheltered courtyard reading may represent that courtyard rather than an unobstructed regional wind. Record the unit, height or site context and averaging interval where supplied. A gust is not the same statistic as a longer-period mean. Comparing one site’s peak gust with another’s mean without explanation can create a misleading contrast.",
      "claim_ids": [
