    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-11",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A wind vane indicates wind direction. The wind pushes the broad tail away, so the arrow head points into the wind, towards the direction the wind is blowing from; it is read against fixed N, E, S and W arms. A northerly wind comes from the north and moves generally towards the south. A diagram must show the cardinal directions or orientation and enough information to distinguish the vane’s pointing end. Do not assume that an arrow on a decorative drawing has the same meaning as a meteorological wind arrow or barb. Read the key and the stated convention before converting the picture into a direction.",
      "claim_ids": [
