    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-12",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "The vane needs to turn freely and be correctly aligned with north. Nearby structures can change local flow, while a stuck vane can produce repeated readings that look consistent but are wrong. Calm conditions may have no meaningful prevailing direction, so a calm record should not automatically inherit the previous direction. If direction changes during an interval, record or summarise it using the stated method rather than inventing one continuous arrow. Wind direction is circular data: a simple arithmetic mean of degrees can fail near north, where 350° and 10° are close rather than opposite.",
