    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-13",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A sunshine recorder measures sunshine duration, not air temperature or total daylight length. A traditional Campbell–Stokes recorder uses a glass sphere to focus sunlight onto a suitable card, leaving a trace during sufficiently bright conditions. Read the burnt sections against the card’s time markings, distinguishing gaps. Modern electronic sensors use other methods. A six-hour sunshine total does not mean the sun was above the horizon for only six hours; clouds and the instrument’s detection conditions affect recorded bright sunshine. The recorder requires an appropriate unobstructed exposure and correct setup for the date and site.",
      "claim_ids": [
