    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-15",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Cloud amount is often estimated by observation rather than one of the instruments just described. A standard school system uses oktas: eighths of the visible sky covered by cloud. Zero indicates clear sky and eight an overcast sky under the stated observation convention. Cloud type describes form and level, which is different from amount. An observation of extensive thin high cloud and one of a small low dark cloud can differ in type and coverage. Do not infer rainfall directly from cloud amount alone; cloud depth, moisture and the wider atmospheric conditions matter.",
