    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-16",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Instrument siting should match purpose. The screen shelters temperature and humidity sensors, the rain gauge collects precipitation openly, the wind instruments need suitable airflow, and the sunshine recorder needs an unobstructed view for its measurement. A station plan should show these functions coherently rather than cluster everything under a tree for protection. If the aim is comparing sheltered and exposed microclimates, non-standard sites may be deliberately selected, but their exposure then becomes part of the investigation and must be recorded. Standardisation and deliberate local comparison are different research designs.",
