      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-19",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A rain-gauge diagram should distinguish the collecting opening from the measuring scale. If a key states that 20 mL in the measuring vessel corresponds to 1 mm of rainfall for that collector, a 120 mL observation represents 6 mm. This stated calibration is a teaching example; it cannot be transferred to another funnel size without justification. The volume in millilitres and rainfall depth in millimetres are related through the collecting area, not interchangeable units.",
