      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.6.03-21",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "For wind instruments, inspect the reference as well as the moving part. A vane can rotate freely but be misaligned with the north marker, producing a consistent directional error. An anemometer can provide speed while supplying no direction on its own. Combining their readings requires the same or clearly related observation interval. A pressure trace, by contrast, uses a time axis, and the pen or sensor location should not be interpreted as a compass bearing. These distinctions help decode an unfamiliar instrument resource from its declared function.",
